# Response headers for Netlify and Cloudflare Pages.
# (Vercel reads vercel.json instead — keep the two in sync.)

/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), interest-cohort=()
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains; preload
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://*.facebook.com https://*.facebook.net https://*.fbcdn.net https://*.instagram.com https://*.cdninstagram.com; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data:; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self' https://*.facebook.com https://*.facebook.net https://*.fbcdn.net https://*.instagram.com https://*.cdninstagram.com; frame-ancestors 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; object-src 'none'; upgrade-insecure-requests

# Note on 'unsafe-inline' in script-src: the theme is applied by a small inline
# script that must run before first paint, and a static site has no server to
# mint a per-request nonce. The policy still blocks every external script
# origin, which is the attack this site is realistically exposed to. If that
# ever stops being good enough, move to hash-based CSP.

# Note on the Meta domains in script-src and connect-src: Facebook and
# Instagram do not hand a link to the phone's browser. They open it in their
# own, and inject their own JavaScript into whatever page it loads — code that
# wraps every link so a tap can be recorded before the page changes. With this
# policy at 'self' the recording half was refused, the wrapper stopped, and the
# navigation it was meant to trigger never ran: inside those two apps every
# link on the site was dead, while the same links worked in Chrome, in X and in
# LinkedIn, which open a real browser instead. Typing a URL by hand worked
# there too, which is what narrowed it to the tap rather than the page.
#
# This allowance is unused everywhere else. The site loads no third-party
# script, font or image, and its own JavaScript makes no cross-origin request
# at all, so no browser but Meta's will ever ask for one of these hosts. It
# also grants Meta nothing new: a browser injecting a script into a page it is
# rendering is outside what a page's own policy can refuse, so their code runs
# with or without this line — the line only lets it finish. What it costs is a
# slice of the safety net, since a script injected into this site by an
# attacker could now reach those hosts. Every other origin is still refused.
#
# Both directives are listed because it was never established which of the two
# was the fatal one, and one deploy to test beat three. Do not narrow or remove
# this without tapping a link inside the Instagram app afterwards — nothing in
# `npm run verify`, and no desktop browser, can see this break.

# Fonts and images are content-hashed by the build, so they can be cached hard.
/_astro/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/fonts/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/og/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
